    "available_at": "2026-11-23T21:53:47.832825+00:00",
    "body": "The Port of Savannah handled 565,366 twenty-foot equivalent units (TEUs) in October, a 25% increase from a year earlier and the second-highest monthly container total in its history.\nThe October gains pushed container volumes for fiscal 2027 to date up 7.8%, an increase of 152,991 TEUs compared with the same period a year earlier, the Georgia Ports Authority said.\nSavannah handled 160 vessels during the month, up 5% year over year.\nContainer volumes at the global top 30 gateway were driven by strong consumer spending, and an extended peak shipping season, said GPA Chief Executive Griff Lynch, in a statement.\nThe increase followed September’s 3.7% year-over-year gain, when Savannah handled 504,015 TEUs amid rising imports and weather-related shipment delays.\nThe cargo gains come as Georgia Ports advances projects designed to expand Savannah’s capacity and improve connections to inland markets.\nIn October the authority announced a $2.3-billion Savannah Container Terminal investment after receiving a permit from the U.S. Army Corps of Engineers . The planned 350-acre facility would provide 3.5 million TEUs of annual capacity, on-dock rail and direct highway access for trucks. Construction is scheduled to begin in 2028, and the authority said the completed project would bring Savannah’s total annual capacity to 12 million TEUs.\nSeparately, the $1.6-billion Ocean Terminal redevelopment is more than 60% complete and will increase the facility’s annual container capacity from 200,000 TEUs to 1.75 million TEUs. GPA said truck gate construction and support services are 99% complete.\nFreightWaves previously reported that the Ocean Terminal project’s first container-yard phase is scheduled for completion in mid-fall 2027, with full yard completion targeted for the end of 2028.\nThe Gainesville Inland Port provides another connection to Savannah, with Norfolk Southern operating direct rail service five days a week between the port and Northeast Georgia. Georgia Ports expects the facility to shift 26,000 containers from highways to rail during its first year.\nSuez routing changes could shorten transit times\nThe return of some ocean carriers to Suez Canal routing is cutting transit times to Savannah by 10 to 12 days as shippers replenish inventory during the holiday shopping season.\nThe authority also cited improving Panama Canal conditions. The canal authority increased daily vessel transits from 33 to 34 effective Nov. 12 and raised the maximum allowable draft at the Neopanamax locks from 49 feet to 50 feet.\nAt the Port of Brunswick, total roll-on/roll-off cargo fell 3.6% year over year to 69,599 units in October. Heavy machinery shipments rose 25.8% to 5,193 units, compared with 4,127 a year earlier. Brunswick is the leading East Coast gateway for vehicle imports.\nA fourth berth at Brunswick’s Colonels Island facility is 60% complete and scheduled to open in November 2027, according to GPA. The authority said the additional berth will help reduce vessel time at berth and improve cargo flows.\nWhy It Matters: The October increase means more containers moving through a Southeast U.S. gateway used by importers, exporters, trucking companies and railroads during the holiday shipping period.\nRead more articles by Stuart Chirls here .\nRead more:\nAsia–US East Coast ocean freight rates top $10,000, up 300% from a year ago\nTentative union deal averts strike at Port of LA-Long Beach\nZim Q3 profit surges to $830 million as freight rates jump 55%\nShip pilot indicted after fatal Houston collision linked to cellphone distraction\nLA-Long Beach port strike risk grows as employers say ILWU talks stalled",
